window.SAVOY_PEOPLE={"generated":"curated + auto index","notable_count":48,"index_count":3232,"notable":[{"id":"william-louis-marshall-savoy","name":"William Louis Marshall Savoy","aka":["Colonel William Savoy","Col. William Savoy","Col. Savoy"],"era":"1850-1899","roles":["town founder","landowner","Confederate soldier"],"summary":"William Louis Marshall Savoy founded the town of Savoy, Texas, in 1872 when he sold forty acres of land to the Transcontinental Railroad (later the Texas and Pacific) on the condition that the new town bear his name. The college history (Boyd, 1939) states he was born in Mississippi in 1818 and came to Texas as a young man, while the memoir 'Before I Remember' — narrated by his daughter — gives his birth year as 1817 and states he died in 1889 at Joshua, Johnson County, Texas, of cancer; Boyd's text says he died August 1880 at Johnson City: these accounts conflict on both the death date and location. He served in the Confederate Army but did not see active service due to a foot wound, and crossed the Atlantic twice and the Pacific three times in later years.","dates":{"born":"c. 1817-1818 (sources conflict)","died":"c. 1880-1889 (sources conflict)"},"contributions":[{"text":"Sold forty acres to the Transcontinental Railroad (Texas and Pacific) in October 1872, founding the town of Savoy and having it named in his honor.","sources":["college_p025"]},{"text":"Filed the city charter on April 15, 1872; sold town lots and donated the lot on which the Methodist Episcopal Church was built.","sources":["college_p026"]},{"text":"Donated the land for the 1878 Methodist Church on Fowler Street, which became one of Savoy's oldest surviving structures.","sources":["2005-news-sanctuarybell","2005-news-sanctuarybell-1"]},{"text":"The 'Before I Remember' memoir (daughter's account) states he was born Aug. 2, 1817, died Sept. 1, 1887 at Goshee, Johnson County; Boyd's college history says born 1818, died August 1880 at Johnson City — a discrepancy in both birth year and death date/place.","sources":["college_p025","college_p026","elk-24","beforeiremember_p1","beforeiremember_p2"]},{"text":"Marriage to Julia E. Davis solemnized March 5, 1874 in Fannin County by minister J. H. Ray.","sources":["williamsavoy-marriagecert"]}],"sources":["college_p025","college_p026","beforeiremember_p1","beforeiremember_p2","beforeiremember_p4","williamsavoy-marriagecert","elk-24","2005-news-sanctuarybell","2005-news-sanctuarybell-1"]},{"id":"julia-e-davis-savoy","name":"Julia E. Davis Savoy","aka":["Mrs. Julia E. Davis","Mrs. William Savoy"],"era":"1850-1899","roles":["spouse of town founder"],"summary":"Julia E. Davis married Colonel William Savoy on March 5, 1874 in Fannin County, Texas. She had three children from a prior marriage to a Dr. Davis (Ernest, Irene, and Emma Marshall). She and Colonel Savoy had four children together including a daughter born November 26, 1874 in Savoy. After her husband's death she survived him by two decades according to Boyd; the memoir narrated by her daughter describes her as a woman of prominence in the Savoy community who recalled the town's welcome reception after their honeymoon.","dates":{"born":"","died":""},"contributions":[{"text":"Married Col. William Savoy on March 5, 1874 in Fannin County, Texas.","sources":["williamsavoy-marriagecert"]},{"text":"Mrs. Savoy survived her husband by two decades according to Boyd; her grave is dated August 7, 1908.","sources":["college_p026"]}],"sources":["williamsavoy-marriagecert","college_p026","beforeiremember_p2","beforeiremember_p4"]},{"id":"ada-savoy-travis","name":"Ada Savoy Travis","aka":["Mrs. Ada Savoy Travis"],"era":"1850-1899","roles":["daughter of town founder","family informant"],"summary":"Ada Savoy Travis was a daughter of Colonel William Savoy and his second wife Tallulah Clark. She was born in Mississippi and received her education in Canton, Mississippi. She served as the primary informant for Boyd's 1939 college history on the subject of her father's life, recounting his California gold rush trip, Civil War service, and extensive travels.","dates":{"born":"","died":""},"contributions":[{"text":"Provided recollections of her father Colonel Savoy's life to author Mattie Lee Boyd, cited as a key source for the biographical chapter.","sources":["college_p025","college_p026"]}],"sources":["college_p025","college_p026"]},{"id":"nancy-savoy","name":"Nancy Savoy","aka":[],"era":"2000-present","roles":["philanthropist","Savoy family descendant"],"summary":"Nancy Savoy, wife of the late John Savoy of Arizona, was the surviving spouse of the last remaining grandson of Colonel William Savoy. She donated $25,000 to Savoy Independent School District around 2004-2005 to fund the removal and restoration of the 1878 Methodist Church bronze bell and to establish a $1,000 annual scholarship. She and her late husband had reconnected with the Savoy community a few years prior to the donation.","dates":{"born":"","died":""},"contributions":[{"text":"Donated $25,000 to Savoy ISD to restore the 1878 Methodist Church bell and fund a $1,000 annual scholarship.","sources":["2005-news-sanctuarybell","2005-news-sanctuarybell-1"]}],"sources":["2005-news-sanctuarybell","2005-news-sanctuarybell-1"]},{"id":"marshall-d-savoy","name":"Marshall D. Savoy","aka":["First Lt. Marshall D. Savoy","Marshall Durey Savoy"],"era":"1900-1949","roles":["military officer","physician","World War II hero"],"summary":"First Lt. Marshall D. Savoy was a Phoenix, Arizona native of the Savoy family and a battalion assistant surgeon in the U.S. Army during World War II. He is identified in family genealogy records as born March 21, 1876 in Savoy, Fannin County, Texas, and died March 21, 1935 in Phoenix. The 1946 Arizona Republic profile describes a Lt. Marshall D. Savoy who joined the regular army in January 1940 and served in the Pacific theater, earning two Silver Stars, the Soldier's Medal, and a Purple Heart with cluster for repeatedly risking his life to aid wounded comrades during the Philippine campaign; this Lt. Savoy may be his son or a closely related family member.","dates":{"born":"1876 (family genealogy) or c. 1920 (WWII-era officer)","died":"1935 (family genealogy)"},"contributions":[{"text":"Awarded two Silver Stars and the Soldier's Medal for heroism under fire as an assistant battalion surgeon during the Philippine campaign, 1945.","sources":["1946-news-marshallsavoy"]},{"text":"Family genealogy form records Marshall Durey Savoy born March 21, 1876 in Savoy, Fannin County, Texas, died March 21, 1935, buried at Greenwood Cemetery, Phoenix, Arizona.","sources":["1876-docs-censusform"]}],"sources":["1946-news-marshallsavoy","1876-docs-censusform"]},{"id":"robert-roberson-halsell","name":"Robert Roberson Halsell","aka":["R. R. Halsell","R. B. Halsell","Prof. Halsell","Professor Halsell","President Halsell"],"era":"1850-1899","roles":["college president","college founder","educator"],"summary":"Robert Roberson Halsell was the founder and president of Savoy Male and Female College, organizing the school in 1876 and guiding it until its closure in the early 1890s. The college received its charter on January 22, 1880, naming Halsell and four co-trustees. The 1932 Sherman newspaper gives his first initial as 'R. H.' while Boyd's 1939 thesis uses 'R. B.' — a minor ambiguity in the sources. Born in Monroe County, Kentucky on September 28, 1848, he taught at Savoy and later in Anson, Texas, then moved to Durant, Oklahoma, where he was elected to the Oklahoma legislature; he died in Durant in 1924.","dates":{"born":"September 28, 1848","died":"1924"},"contributions":[{"text":"Founded Savoy College in 1876 as a local school; expanded it to a chartered institution receiving its formal charter on January 22, 1880.","sources":["1932-news-halsell","college_p160"]},{"text":"Led the college through its most celebrated period; known for inspiring students with Latin phrases and 'fireside chat' speeches on self-improvement.","sources":["college_p167","college_p196"]},{"text":"Elected to the Oklahoma legislature after leaving Savoy; received a congratulatory letter signed by former students and prominent Grayson County citizens.","sources":["college_p165","college_p166"]},{"text":"Taught in Anson, Texas 1890-1892 after leaving Savoy, then returned briefly before continuing educational work in Oklahoma.","sources":["college_p163"]},{"text":"Died in Durant, Oklahoma in 1924; survived by daughters Mrs. Annie Woodward of Durant and Mrs. Mollie Durant of Calera, Oklahoma, and son Doak Halsell.","sources":["1932-news-halsell"]}],"sources":["college_p151","college_p160","college_p161","college_p162","college_p163","college_p164","college_p165","college_p166","college_p196","1932-news-halsell","beforeiremember_p4","college_p258"]},{"id":"lewis-holland","name":"Lewis Holland","aka":["Reverend Lewis Holland","Professor Holland","Rev. Lewis Holland"],"era":"1850-1899","roles":["college vice-president","professor","Baptist minister","newspaper founder"],"summary":"Lewis Holland was vice-president and co-principal of Savoy College, serving from the fall of 1886 to the spring of 1889. Born November 8, 1840, in Maury County, Tennessee, he served in Company D, Fourteenth Arkansas Confederate Infantry during the Civil War. He taught Latin, French, and higher mathematics at Savoy for ten or twelve years. He was one of the founders of the Baptist News (later the Baptist Standard), a major Texas Baptist newspaper, and served as vice-president of the State Baptist Convention, vice-president of Howard College, and president of the Board of Florence University. He died in San Antonio in 1914 at age 74.","dates":{"born":"November 8, 1840","died":"1914"},"contributions":[{"text":"Co-founded the Baptist News at Honey Grove in December 1886 with J. H. Haines; the paper evolved into the Baptist Standard.","sources":["college_p169","college_p170"]},{"text":"Taught Latin, French, and higher mathematics at Savoy College for 10-12 years; served as vice-president under President Halsell.","sources":["1932-news-halsell","college_p168"]},{"text":"Served as faculty at Savoy from fall 1886 to spring 1889; replaced by Haskell.","sources":["college_p171"]},{"text":"After Savoy, moved to Whitewright, then San Antonio, then Oklahoma; inherited wealth and died in 1914 age 74.","sources":["1932-news-halsell"]}],"sources":["college_p168","college_p169","college_p170","college_p171","1932-news-halsell","college_p179"]},{"id":"james-l-german","name":"James L. German","aka":["J. L. German"],"era":"1850-1899","roles":["college trustee","founder","farmer","educator"],"summary":"James L. German was one of three founding trustees of Savoy Male and Female College, named in its January 22, 1880 charter. Born May 8, 1833, in Mercer County, Missouri, of Welsh descent, he taught school from the age of seventeen and earned his higher education at the University of Missouri. He came to Texas in 1874, married former pupil Eliza Paxton (daughter of Samuel S. Paxton), and became a prosperous farmer near Savoy. He helped finance many young people's educations, was a lifelong total abstainer, and was a supporter of Senator Joseph W. Bailey. He and his wife Eliza German had no children.","dates":{"born":"May 8, 1833","died":""},"contributions":[{"text":"Named as one of five original charter trustees of Savoy Male and Female College, charter granted January 22, 1880.","sources":["1932-news-halsell","college_p152"]},{"text":"Taught school starting at age seventeen; funded his higher education at the University of Missouri through teaching.","sources":["college_p152","college_p153"]},{"text":"Moved to Texas in 1874; farmed near Savoy and helped finance the education of many aspiring youth.","sources":["college_p154","college_p156"]}],"sources":["college_p151","college_p152","college_p153","college_p154","college_p156","1932-news-halsell"]},{"id":"thomas-j-chenoweth","name":"Thomas J. Chenoweth","aka":["T. J. Chenoweth"],"era":"1850-1899","roles":["college trustee","founder","farmer"],"summary":"Thomas J. Chenoweth was one of three founding trustees of Savoy Male and Female College, named in its January 22, 1880 charter. He was the son of Dr. John W. Chenoweth and grew up in southwestern Missouri before coming to Texas. In 1874 or 1876 he married a sister of James Paxton and a sister of Mrs. German, creating a close-knit family network among the college's founders. He was a devoted member of the Christian Church and a strong supporter of Christian education. His daughter Frankie was a piano teacher at the college in the late 1880s, and his daughter Tommie also taught in the Savoy schools.","dates":{"born":"","died":""},"contributions":[{"text":"Named as one of five original charter trustees of Savoy Male and Female College, charter granted January 22, 1880.","sources":["1932-news-halsell","college_p151"]},{"text":"Son Frankie (daughter) taught piano at the College in the late 1880s; daughter Tommie also taught in Savoy schools.","sources":["college_p157"]}],"sources":["college_p151","college_p152","college_p157","1932-news-halsell"]},{"id":"james-paxton","name":"James Paxton","aka":["Mr. Paxton"],"era":"1850-1899","roles":["college trustee","founder","farmer","stock breeder"],"summary":"James Paxton was one of three founding trustees of Savoy Male and Female College. Born November 18, 1833, the fifteenth child of William Casey and Mary Paxton of Kentucky, he came from a distinguished military family — his grandfather organized a company for General Jackson at New Orleans in 1814, and his father commanded volunteers in the Mexican War and served in the Kentucky legislature. He moved to Savoy around 1860-1870, became a fine stock breeder, and acquired about two thousand acres. He was a lifelong member of the Christian Church. He died April 10, 1914, in Tulsa (or Haskell), Texas, nearly 81 years old.","dates":{"born":"November 18, 1833","died":"April 10, 1914"},"contributions":[{"text":"Named as one of five original charter trustees of Savoy Male and Female College, charter granted January 22, 1880.","sources":["1932-news-halsell","college_p151"]},{"text":"Settled in Savoy around 1860-1870; became a prominent stock breeder and farmer.","sources":["college_p159"]},{"text":"Died April 10, 1914 at nearly 81 years of age.","sources":["college_p160"]}],"sources":["college_p151","college_p158","college_p159","college_p160","1932-news-halsell"]},{"id":"richard-a-gay","name":"Richard A. Gay","aka":["Dick Gay","R. A. Gay","Rev. R. A. Gay"],"era":"1850-1899","roles":["professor","minister"],"summary":"Richard A. Gay, universally known as 'Dick' Gay, was one of the earliest faculty members at Savoy College. He came to Texas from Georgia at age four and attended public schools in Fannin County before studying at Savoy College. He conducted the Primary Department of the College as early as 1881-1882 and taught several years. He was ordained as a minister in 1880 at Ladonia, Fannin County. For the 1885-1886 school year he served on the faculty of the State Normal College at Athens, Texas, teaching mathematics. He died of heart failure, reportedly at the end of March 1887.","dates":{"born":"","died":"c. March 1887"},"contributions":[{"text":"Conducted the Primary Department of Savoy College as early as 1881-1882; taught several years while also advancing his own studies.","sources":["college_p172"]},{"text":"Served on faculty at the State Normal College at Athens, Texas in 1885-1886 as teacher of mathematics.","sources":["college_p172"]}],"sources":["college_p172","college_p174"]},{"id":"edward-h-pritchett","name":"Edward H. Pritchett","aka":["E. H. Pritchett","Professor Pritchett"],"era":"1850-1899","roles":["professor","civil engineer","lawyer"],"summary":"Edward H. Pritchett was the oldest of five children born December 24, 1824 in Fayette County, Georgia. He attended public schools in Fannin County, continued at Savoy College, then graduated from Alabama Polytechnic Institute in January 1879 with a B.E. degree. After the destruction of Savoy College, he took up the study of law and was admitted to the Texas bar. He served many years as steward and Sunday School superintendent at Bonham and was a member of the Methodist Episcopal Church. He died June 9, 1925.","dates":{"born":"December 24, 1824","died":"June 9, 1925"},"contributions":[{"text":"Graduated from Alabama Polytechnic Institute in 1880 with a B.E. degree; taught at Savoy College before pursuing law and civil engineering.","sources":["college_p176"]},{"text":"Died June 9, 1925 at Bonham, Texas after long career as steward and Sunday School superintendent at Bonham Methodist Episcopal Church.","sources":["college_p176"]}],"sources":["college_p174","college_p176"]},{"id":"edward-p-montgomery","name":"Edward P. Montgomery","aka":["Pink Montgomery","E. P. Montgomery","Dr. Edward P. Montgomery"],"era":"1850-1899","roles":["professor","physician"],"summary":"Edward P. ('Pink') Montgomery was a brief but well-remembered faculty member at Savoy College. Born in Pikesville, Tennessee, he came to Texas in 1868 and attended Savoy Male and Female College under President Halsell. In 1883 he graduated with honors from Vanderbilt University Medical School. He commenced practice as a physician and became a rising figure among Texas surgeons. His long service as a physician in Grayson County ended in a train wreck at Whitesboro; his widow died in 1925.","dates":{"born":"c. 1848","died":""},"contributions":[{"text":"Graduated with honors from Vanderbilt University in 1883; practiced medicine in Grayson County, Texas.","sources":["college_p177"]},{"text":"Attended Savoy Male and Female College under President Halsell; briefly served on faculty as a teacher.","sources":["college_p177"]},{"text":"His son Crawford Blair Montgomery was connected to the college history; he died in a train wreck at Whitesboro.","sources":["college_p178"]}],"sources":["college_p175","college_p177","college_p178"]},{"id":"john-thomas-ashcraft","name":"John Thomas Ashcraft","aka":["J. T. Ashcraft","Mr. Ashcraft"],"era":"1850-1899","roles":["professor","lawyer","public health advocate"],"summary":"John Thomas Ashcraft was a faculty member at Savoy College and Polytechnic College who became one of the more prominent alumni in public life. Born in Clay County, Alabama, he came to Texas early in life, graduated from Polytechnic, and studied medicine in Louisville, earning his medical degree in 1887. He married Miss Aggie McKee on December 16, 1885. He taught mathematics and natural science at both Polytechnic College and Savoy Male and Female College. He was a member of the State Anti-Tuberculosis Commission and undertook major public health campaigns until his death on February 9, 1920. Ten children were born to him and Mrs. Ashcraft.","dates":{"born":"","died":"February 9, 1920"},"contributions":[{"text":"Served on the State Anti-Tuberculosis Commission and led public health campaigns until his death.","sources":["college_p181"]},{"text":"Taught higher mathematics, natural science, and education at Polytechnic College and Savoy Male and Female College.","sources":["college_p178","college_p180"]},{"text":"Died February 9, 1920; son Cyrus Andrew was at one time a vice-president of United States Steel Corporation.","sources":["college_p181"]}],"sources":["college_p178","college_p179","college_p180","college_p181","college_p182"]},{"id":"leonidas-william-pierce","name":"Leonidas William Pierce","aka":["L. W. Pierce","Lee Pierce","Professor W. Pierce"],"era":"1850-1899","roles":["professor","missionary","student"],"summary":"Leonidas William Pierce was born October 15, 1870, to Sidney Pierce and Cornelia Brown. He attended Savoy College as a student, earned his A.B. degree in 1883, and was immediately appointed to the faculty to teach English, German, and later natural science. He served as senior assistant editor and business manager of the student paper, the Philomean Messenger. He completed his theological training at the Southern Baptist Theological Seminary in Louisville, Kentucky in 1892 and was sent as a missionary to Yangchow, China. He married Miss Molly E. Tupper in 1893 and spent thirty-one years as a missionary in China, establishing two churches in Yangchow and twelve others in nearby cities. He drowned July 16, 1922, at Soochow, China when a launch capsized, and is buried at Chinkiang.","dates":{"born":"October 15, 1870","died":"July 16, 1922"},"contributions":[{"text":"Received A.B. degree from Savoy College in 1883; appointed to faculty as teacher of English, German, and natural science.","sources":["college_p182","college_p183"]},{"text":"Served as missionary in Yangchow, China for thirty-one years, establishing two churches in Yangchow and twelve others in surrounding cities and towns.","sources":["college_p185"]},{"text":"Drowned July 16, 1922 at Soochow, China; buried at Chinkiang, China.","sources":["college_p185"]}],"sources":["college_p182","college_p183","college_p184","college_p185"]},{"id":"will-i-hunt","name":"Will I. Hunt","aka":["W. I. Hunt","Will Hunt"],"era":"1850-1899","roles":["professor","student","county official"],"summary":"Will I. Hunt was born May 9, 1861, near the present town of Whitewright, Texas. He entered Savoy College in 1882, excelled in the Victorian Literary Society, and served as editor of the Philomean Messenger in his final year. He graduated with an A.B. degree in spring 1887 — the second alumnus of Savoy College to complete the A.B. course — and was invited to join the teaching staff, teaching Greek and higher mathematics. He later moved to Stephenville, Texas where he served over forty years in various county offices including County Surveyor, County Clerk, District Clerk, and County Commissioner. He was, per the 1939 thesis, the only surviving member of the regular College faculty at the time of writing.","dates":{"born":"May 9, 1861","died":""},"contributions":[{"text":"Second alumnus of Savoy College to complete the A.B. degree (1887); immediately joined faculty as instructor in Greek and higher mathematics.","sources":["college_p185","college_p186"]},{"text":"Served as County Surveyor, County Clerk, District Clerk, and County Commissioner in Stephenville, Texas over more than forty years.","sources":["college_p186"]},{"text":"Married Miss Mollie Lee on August 9, 1888; celebrated their golden wedding anniversary on August 9, 1938.","sources":["college_p186"]}],"sources":["college_p185","college_p186","college_p187","college_p179"]},{"id":"elbu-bennett-hinshaw","name":"Elbu Bennett Hinshaw","aka":["E. B. Hinshaw","Elbu B. Hinshaw","Professor Hinshaw"],"era":"1850-1899","roles":["professor","educator","seminary superintendent"],"summary":"Elbu Bennett Hinshaw was born October 14, 1860, in Indiana, of Pennsylvania Quaker ancestry. He graduated from Hiwasee College in Tennessee in 1884 with a B.S. degree and received an M.A. from the same institution. He joined the Savoy College faculty in early 1887 to fill the vacancy in classical languages left by Professor Holland's retirement. In 1887 he was selected to become president of Bloomfield Seminary in Panola County, Chickasaw Nation, an educational institution for young Indian women. He remained superintendent for eighteen years, married twice, and later taught at Southeastern State Normal School at Durant. He was a close friend of Governor Johnston, the last chief of the Chickasaw Nation. He died in Oklahoma City, June 27, 1920, and was buried in Collinsville, Texas.","dates":{"born":"October 14, 1860","died":"June 27, 1920"},"contributions":[{"text":"Joined Savoy College faculty in 1887 to teach classical languages; served from 1887 to near the college's closure.","sources":["college_p187"]},{"text":"Became superintendent of Bloomfield Seminary in the Chickasaw Nation in 1887, serving eighteen years and building it to 220 students.","sources":["college_p187","college_p188"]},{"text":"Died June 27, 1920 in Oklahoma City; buried in Collinsville, Texas.","sources":["college_p188"]}],"sources":["college_p187","college_p188"]},{"id":"alexander-c-bohannon","name":"Alexander C. Bohannon","aka":["A. C. Bohannon","Professor Bohannon"],"era":"1850-1899","roles":["professor","educator"],"summary":"Alexander C. Bohannon was a Virginian born July 22, 1820, in Mathews County, Virginia, the son of Reuben W. and Nancy Bohannon. He was a graduate of Hampden-Sidney College and studied law before the Civil War, during which he served in the Confederate cavalry. He taught at schools in North Carolina, Indiana, Kentucky, and various Texas institutions before being invited by Professor Holland to fill the chair of logic and rhetoric at Savoy College around 1881. He remained at Savoy until the college closed in 1890, then served as superintendent of schools at Bells, Texas for two years. He died around 1895 or 1896. His daughter Hattie Donovan Bohannon authored the novel 'A Second Marriage' (G. W. Dillingham, New York, 1909).","dates":{"born":"July 22, 1820","died":"c. 1895-1896"},"contributions":[{"text":"Filled chair of logic and rhetoric at Savoy College from approximately 1881 through the college's closure in 1890.","sources":["college_p189","college_p190"]},{"text":"After Savoy College closed in 1890, served as superintendent of schools at Bells, Texas for two years.","sources":["college_p190"]}],"sources":["college_p189","college_p190"]},{"id":"l-j-reynolds","name":"Leonidas J. Reynolds","aka":["L. J. Reynolds","Lewis J. Reynolds","Leon Reynolds"],"era":"1850-1899","roles":["student","professor","printer","newspaper publisher"],"summary":"L. J. Reynolds was born November 6, 1864 in Livingston, Polk County, Texas. He enrolled in Savoy College shortly after the devastating 1880 tornado, and through years of part-time work and teaching he earned his B.S. degree in 1887 with the help and encouragement of President Halsell. He began his career in a Sherman newspaper office, then in August 1892 established the Van Alstyne Leader newspaper. After several ventures he managed the Guaranty Printing Company in Dallas from 1923 onward. He was a founding board member of the 1942 publication of Boyd's college history and served as one of four Board of Managers members responsible for its publication. His personal sketch forms the conclusion of Chapter VI in Boyd's work.","dates":{"born":"November 6, 1864","died":""},"contributions":[{"text":"Graduated with B.S. from Savoy College in 1887 after seven years of study while working to support his family.","sources":["college_p196"]},{"text":"Founded the Van Alstyne Leader newspaper in August 1892; later managed the Guaranty Printing Company in Dallas.","sources":["college_p197"]},{"text":"Served on the Board of Managers that published Boyd's college history in 1942.","sources":["college_p256"]}],"sources":["college_p194","college_p195","college_p196","college_p197","college_p198","college_p256"]},{"id":"mattie-lee-boyd","name":"Mattie Lee Boyd","aka":["Miss Mattie Lee Boyd"],"era":"1900-1949","roles":["author","historian","graduate researcher"],"summary":"Mattie Lee Boyd authored the thesis 'The Savoy Male and Female College, 1876-1890: Its Organization, Administration, History, and Tradition,' presented to the Faculty of the Graduate School of the University of Texas in partial fulfillment of the requirements for a Master of Arts degree in June 1939. The Ex-Student Association sponsored its publication in 1942. Her research involved extensive correspondence with surviving ex-students and faculty, and she was gifted an unstrung bow by former Indian students as a symbol of peace. Boyd refused to allow her photograph in the book, but the publishers included it anyway as a tribute.","dates":{"born":"","died":""},"contributions":[{"text":"Wrote 1939 M.A. thesis on Savoy College at the University of Texas; published in 1942 by the Savoy College Ex-Student Association.","sources":["college_p007","college_p256"]},{"text":"Received an unstrung bow as a gift from former Indian students; the bow was placed in the archives of the Association as a sacred relic.","sources":["college_p257"]}],"sources":["college_p007","college_p256","college_p257"]},{"id":"e-a-mcmahon","name":"Edgar A. McMahon","aka":["E. A. McMahon","Ed. A. McMahon","Ed McMahon"],"era":"1900-1949","roles":["college reunion organizer","board president","alumni leader","postal worker"],"summary":"Edgar A. McMahon was a prominent Savoy College alumnus who served as chairman of the General Reunion Committee for the 1938 reunion, and as president of the Board of Managers that published the 1942 college history. He provided first-hand recollections of Indian students at the college. He was a rural mail carrier on Route 3 out of Savoy in earlier years, and his wife was the former Lillie Chesworth. By the time of the 1938 reunion, he lived in Dallas.","dates":{"born":"","died":""},"contributions":[{"text":"Chaired the General Reunion Committee for the 1938 Savoy College reunion and served as President of the Board of Managers that published Boyd's history in 1942.","sources":["college_p204","college_p256"]},{"text":"Provided personal recollections of Indian students at Savoy College to author Mattie Lee Boyd.","sources":["college_p135"]},{"text":"Served as rural mail carrier on Route 3 out of Savoy; wrote a column about mail handling in The Elk.","sources":["elk-14"]}],"sources":["college_p135","college_p204","college_p210","college_p256","elk-14"]},{"id":"gus-w-thomasson","name":"Gus W. Thomasson","aka":["Gus Thomasson"],"era":"1900-1949","roles":["reunion organizer","WPA foreman","alumni leader"],"summary":"Gus W. Thomasson was a grandson of Thomas J. Chenoweth, one of the founding trustees of Savoy College. He was working as a WPA foreman on construction of the new Savoy school gymnasium in 1937 when he initiated the effort to reunite former Savoy College students. On August 15, 1937, he sent a circular letter to a mailing list of former students, sparking a chain of events that led to the 1938 reunion and ultimately to the 1942 publication of Boyd's history. He served on the Board of Managers that published the history.","dates":{"born":"","died":""},"contributions":[{"text":"Sent the August 15, 1937 circular letter that initiated the Savoy College reunion movement, leading to the 1938 reunion and 1942 published history.","sources":["college_p203"]},{"text":"Served on the Board of Managers of the Savoy College Ex-Student Association responsible for publishing Boyd's history in 1942.","sources":["college_p256"]}],"sources":["college_p203","college_p210","college_p256"]},{"id":"beulah-thomasson-stinnett","name":"Beulah Thomasson Stinnett","aka":["Mrs. J. A. Stinnett","Mrs. Beulah Thomasson Stinnett"],"era":"1900-1949","roles":["alumni leader","reunion organizer"],"summary":"Beulah Thomasson Stinnett served on the Board of Managers of the Savoy College Ex-Student Association that published Boyd's 1942 college history. She lived in Van Alstyne at the time of the 1938 reunion and was an active participant in reunion organization. She is listed among the underwriters who contributed to the printing fund for the book.","dates":{"born":"","died":""},"contributions":[{"text":"Served on the Board of Managers of the Savoy College Ex-Student Association that published Boyd's college history in 1942.","sources":["college_p256"]}],"sources":["college_p210","college_p256"]},{"id":"smith-paul","name":"Smith Paul","aka":[],"era":"1850-1899","roles":["Indian student","newspaper publisher","stock dealer"],"summary":"Smith Paul was a Chickasaw Indian student at Savoy College who went on to become a newspaper publisher. His father was a member of the Chickasaw Nation who settled in Paul's Valley in the Indian Territory; his mother was Lucy White of the Chickasaw tribe. After completing his literary course at Paul's Valley and a business course at Mahon's Business College in Dallas in the 1880s, he became proprietor of the Weekly Enterprise, published at Paul's Valley. He also served as deputy collector and was involved in farming and stock-raising.","dates":{"born":"","died":""},"contributions":[{"text":"Attended Savoy College as a Chickasaw Nation student; completed a business course at Mahon's Business College in Dallas in the 1880s.","sources":["college_p137"]},{"text":"Became proprietor of the Weekly Enterprise newspaper at Paul's Valley after 1900.","sources":["college_p137","college_p139"]},{"text":"Served as deputy collector and was active in farming and stock-raising in the Chickasaw Nation.","sources":["college_p137"]}],"sources":["college_p137","college_p138","college_p139"]},{"id":"will-paul","name":"Will Paul","aka":[],"era":"1850-1899","roles":["Indian student","Chickasaw Nation member"],"summary":"Will Paul was a Chickasaw Indian and brother of Smith Paul, both identified by Boyd as students at Savoy College who went on to noteworthy distinction. The Paul brothers' father was a man of the Chickasaw Nation who became a prominent farmer and stock-dealer and served in the legislature and senate of the Indian Territory. The sources conflate some details of Smith and Will Paul's biographies due to scanning artifacts.","dates":{"born":"","died":""},"contributions":[{"text":"Attended Savoy College as a Chickasaw Nation student; identified by Boyd as one of the Indian students marked for noteworthy distinction.","sources":["college_p137"]}],"sources":["college_p137","college_p138"]},{"id":"moses-chipley","name":"Moses Chipley","aka":[],"era":"1850-1899","roles":["Indian student","lawyer","legislator","county attorney"],"summary":"Moses Chipley was a prominent Chickasaw Indian student at Savoy College, also educated at Franklin, Tennessee. His photograph and sketch appear in the introduction to Boyd's college history. He became a prominent lawyer in Davis, Indian Territory, serving in the legislature and holding the office of County Attorney. He was also connected with the Ivan Trading Company and was active in stock-raising in the Chickasaw Nation. His father was a noted leader in the Chickasaw Nation.","dates":{"born":"","died":""},"contributions":[{"text":"Attended Savoy College and Franklin, Tennessee; became a prominent lawyer in Davis, Indian Territory.","sources":["college_p141"]},{"text":"Served in the legislature and as County Attorney; connected with the Ivan Trading Company in the Chickasaw Nation.","sources":["college_p141"]}],"sources":["college_p140","college_p141"]},{"id":"frank-anderson","name":"Frank Anderson","aka":[],"era":"1850-1899","roles":["Indian student","peace officer","U.S. Deputy Marshal"],"summary":"Frank Anderson was a full-blood Choctaw Indian, born August 20, 1877 in Tishomingo, Chickasaw Nation, Indian Territory. He was sent to Savoy College in 1886 at the age of thirteen, unable to speak English on arrival. He attended the college from 1886 to 1889, when the building burned, and then transferred to Spencer Academy near Hugo, Oklahoma. After his education he became an interpreter for the U.S. Court and doorkeeper for the Chickasaw Legislature, a deputy U.S. Marshal under R. P. Taylor, and a deputy sheriff in Bryan County, Oklahoma. His personal letter to Boyd (August 4, 1938) describes his Savoy experience and subsequent career as a peace officer.","dates":{"born":"August 20, 1877","died":""},"contributions":[{"text":"Attended Savoy College 1886-1889 as a full-blood Choctaw Indian; learned English while a student.","sources":["college_p144","college_p145"]},{"text":"After college served as U.S. Court interpreter, Chickasaw Legislature doorkeeper, deputy U.S. Marshal, and deputy sheriff in Bryan County, Oklahoma.","sources":["college_p144","college_p145"]}],"sources":["college_p141","college_p144","college_p145"]},{"id":"george-w-truett","name":"George W. Truett","aka":["G. W. Truett","Dr. George W. Truett"],"era":"1850-1899","roles":["student debater","Baptist pastor","church statesman"],"summary":"George W. Truett participated as a student debater on April 18, 1890, representing Whitewright school on the negative side of a debate against Savoy school held at Whitewright. He later became the world-famous pastor of First Baptist Church in Dallas and a noted church statesman. In a letter to Boyd, he recalled the debate warmly and praised the influence of Savoy College under President Robert R. Halsell, writing: 'There is literally no computation of the far-reaching good done by such schools as the ones at Savoy and Whitewright a generation and more ago.'","dates":{"born":"","died":""},"contributions":[{"text":"Participated in the April 18, 1890 inter-school debate between Savoy and Whitewright as a student representing Whitewright on the negative side.","sources":["college_p258"]},{"text":"Later became pastor of First Baptist Church in Dallas and a world-renowned church statesman; praised Savoy College in a letter to Boyd.","sources":["college_p258"]}],"sources":["college_p258"]},{"id":"t-e-arterberry","name":"T. E. Arterberry","aka":["T. E. Arterberry","Arterberry"],"era":"1850-1899","roles":["newspaper publisher","Savoy Star founder"],"summary":"T. E. Arterberry was the founder and longtime publisher of the Savoy Star newspaper, which he operated along with his partner L. H. King from around 1902. He bought out King and continued publishing the Star for approximately 41 years until his death. He was born and raised in Savoy and received his education at Savoy College. After his death, his wife Mrs. J. B. May (née Martha Buford) continued operating the paper until its suspension with the June 25 issue (year uncertain). The century-old Washington hand press on which the Star was printed was subsequently purchased by the Sherman Democrat as a museum piece.","dates":{"born":"","died":""},"contributions":[{"text":"Founded the Savoy Star newspaper (originally Savoy Tribune) around 1890-1902; operated it for approximately 41 years.","sources":["elk-24","elk-26"]},{"text":"The Savoy Star's historic Washington hand press, made around 1830, was purchased by the Sherman Democrat after publication ceased.","sources":["elk-26"]}],"sources":["elk-24","elk-26"]},{"id":"mrs-j-b-may","name":"Martha Buford May","aka":["Mrs. J. B. May","Miss Marsha Buford","Mrs. T. E. Arterberry"],"era":"1900-1949","roles":["newspaper publisher","Savoy Star operator","local historian"],"summary":"Martha Buford May, known as Mrs. J. B. May, continued operating the Savoy Star after the death of its founder T. E. Arterberry, her first husband. Her daughter or she herself had been a teacher at the Savoy school. She was deeply committed to preserving local history, attempting through the newspaper to save community records and scrapbooks. She served on the General Reunion Committee for the 1938 Savoy College reunion and is listed as an underwriter of Boyd's 1942 published history. She was also an informant for Boyd's research.","dates":{"born":"","died":""},"contributions":[{"text":"Continued operating the Savoy Star after founder T. E. Arterberry's death, maintaining it until publication suspended.","sources":["elk-26"]},{"text":"Served on General Reunion Committee for the 1938 Savoy College reunion.","sources":["college_p204"]},{"text":"Committed to preserving local Savoy history through the newspaper; aided by community scrapbook compilers.","sources":["elk-24"]}],"sources":["elk-24","elk-26","college_p204","college_p256"]},{"id":"w-p-carter","name":"W. P. Carter","aka":["W. F. Carter"],"era":"1900-1949","roles":["Savoy postmaster","school board president"],"summary":"W. P. Carter (also referred to as W. F. Carter) served as postmaster of Savoy and as president of the Savoy School Board. He had been a student at the old Savoy school and was supportive of the 1937-1938 college reunion effort. Sources in The Elk list 'W. P. Carter' as the Savoy postmaster and note a 'Big Price Concession sale' at his store. The school board president who worked with Gus W. Thomasson on the reunion is referred to as W. F. Carter, president of the Savoy School Board.","dates":{"born":"","died":""},"contributions":[{"text":"Served as president of Savoy School Board and co-organized the 1937-1938 college reunion effort with WPA foreman Gus W. Thomasson.","sources":["college_p203"]},{"text":"Served as postmaster of Savoy for multiple terms; held a general store in Savoy.","sources":["elk-24","elk-5"]}],"sources":["college_p203","college_p210","elk-5","elk-24"]},{"id":"lou-arterberry","name":"Lou Arterberry","aka":["Miss Lou Arterberry","Lou A. Arterberry"],"era":"1900-1949","roles":["student","informant","community historian"],"summary":"Lou Arterberry was an ex-student of Savoy College who provided Boyd with extensive personal recollections, including vivid descriptions of Madame Michely (Spanish music teacher) and of preparatory teacher John S. Barratt. She was a lifelong resident of Savoy who helped preserve college records. She is listed as an underwriter of Boyd's 1942 published history, and the old Savoy College bell survived the fire and was in her possession, used as a doorstop.","dates":{"born":"","died":""},"contributions":[{"text":"Provided author Boyd with personal recollections of Savoy College faculty including descriptions of Madame Michely and John S. Barratt.","sources":["college_p190","college_p192"]},{"text":"Preserved a photograph of the college bell and used it as a doorstop after the college burned; listed as underwriter of 1942 published history.","sources":["elk-24","college_p256"]}],"sources":["college_p190","college_p192","college_p256","elk-24"]},{"id":"tom-w-mcspadden","name":"Tom W. McSpadden","aka":["Thomas McSpadden"],"era":"1850-1899","roles":["pioneer settler","farmer"],"summary":"Tom W. McSpadden was an early Savoy pioneer, born September 22, 1849, in Monroe County, Tennessee. He came to Texas at the age of 21 and settled where Savoy now is when only three small shacks stood on the prairie. He began by splitting rails and then farmed for the rest of his working life. He married Miss Margaret Benton on June 5, 1872 in Texas — the same year Savoy was founded — and they celebrated their sixtieth wedding anniversary in 1932. He and his wife were both members of the Methodist Church and had six children.","dates":{"born":"September 22, 1849","died":""},"contributions":[{"text":"Arrived in the Savoy area as one of the earliest settlers, when only three small shacks stood on the prairie; farmed there his entire life.","sources":["1932-news-mcspadden"]},{"text":"Married June 5, 1872 in Texas — the year of Savoy's founding; celebrated their 60th anniversary in 1932.","sources":["1932-news-mcspadden"]}],"sources":["1932-news-mcspadden"]},{"id":"margaret-benton-mcspadden","name":"Margaret Benton McSpadden","aka":["Mrs. Tom McSpadden","Miss Margaret Benton"],"era":"1850-1899","roles":["pioneer settler"],"summary":"Margaret Benton McSpadden was born November 22, 1850 in McMinn County, Tennessee, the daughter of Mr. and Mrs. J. F. Benton and one of fourteen children. She married Tom W. McSpadden on June 5, 1872 in Texas and settled near Savoy where they farmed all their lives. She and her husband were Savoy pioneers and celebrated their sixtieth wedding anniversary in 1932. She was a member of the Methodist Church and suffered from rheumatism in her later years.","dates":{"born":"November 22, 1850","died":""},"contributions":[{"text":"Settled near Savoy in 1872 after marriage; longtime pioneer resident and farmer's wife.","sources":["1932-news-mcspadden"]}],"sources":["1932-news-mcspadden"]},{"id":"harold-ballard","name":"Harold Ballard","aka":["Councilman Harold Ballard"],"era":"2000-present","roles":["city councilman","teacher"],"summary":"Harold Ballard was a Savoy city councilman and former high school science teacher who died in office on February 19, 2002. He was elected to the City Council the previous May (2001) and collapsed at a council meeting table while discussion of a funding cut for the volunteer fire department was underway. He was pronounced dead a short time later at Wilson N. Jones Medical Center. City Hall was closed Wednesday and the flag flown at half-staff. Firefighter R. L. Munger witnessed the collapse and another firefighter administered CPR.","dates":{"born":"","died":"February 19, 2002"},"contributions":[{"text":"Served as Savoy city councilman from May 2001; died in office on February 19, 2002, during a city council meeting.","sources":["2002-news-haroldballard"]}],"sources":["2002-news-haroldballard"]},{"id":"eula-phillips","name":"Eula Phillips","aka":[],"era":"1950-1999","roles":["newspaper stringer","U.S. Army drill sergeant","civic advocate","landlady"],"summary":"Eula Phillips was a lifelong Savoy-area resident who served as a drill sergeant in the U.S. Army and later became a newspaper stringer for the Sherman Democrat, covering Savoy community news for approximately twenty-six years. She was known for her fierce advocacy of open government and transparent school district and city operations. A woman of independent means, she owned rental properties and dozens of pecan trees. She died at the age of 90. Her colleague at the Herald Democrat, Kathy Williams, wrote a tribute column describing her as 'tough-as-nails' but warm-hearted.","dates":{"born":"c. 1917","died":"2007"},"contributions":[{"text":"Served as a drill sergeant in the U.S. Army; later became a longtime newspaper stringer for the Sherman Democrat covering Savoy civic affairs.","sources":["2007-news-eulaphillips"]},{"text":"Fierce advocate for open government; confronted secrecy in school district and city operations for approximately 26 years.","sources":["2007-news-eulaphillips"]}],"sources":["2007-news-eulaphillips"]},{"id":"jim-ralph-elk","name":"Jim Ralph Elk","aka":["Jim Elk"],"era":"1950-1999","roles":["NASA physicist","space scientist"],"summary":"Jim Ralph Elk of Friendswood, Texas, near the NASA Space Center in Houston, was a Savoy-connected physicist who headed Lunar Trajectories Studies at NASA's Space Flight Center for at least eight years. He graduated from East Texas State University with both a B.S. and M.S. in Physics. Before NASA, he worked on cryogenic aeronautical rocket engines for the Lewis Research Institute in Cleveland, Ohio. He was an active Scout leader and his family visited Savoy and Bonham regularly. His mother, Mrs. R. C. Elk, lived in Savoy.","dates":{"born":"","died":""},"contributions":[{"text":"Headed Lunar Trajectories Studies at NASA Space Flight Center; designed and mapped mathematical procedures for lunar landing trajectory developments.","sources":["jimelk-1"]}],"sources":["jimelk","jimelk-1"]},{"id":"brian-neal","name":"Brian Neal","aka":[],"era":"2000-present","roles":["school superintendent","civic leader"],"summary":"Brian Neal served as superintendent of Savoy Independent School District in the early 2000s. He led the effort to safely remove the 1,000-pound bronze bell from the deteriorating 1878 Methodist Church tower on Fowler Street, working with donor Nancy Savoy and community members. He was instrumental in preserving the bell — one of Savoy's oldest artifacts — and in planning for its relocation to the school as a scholarship memorial.","dates":{"born":"","died":""},"contributions":[{"text":"Led the 2005 removal and preservation of the 1878 Methodist Church bell, working with Nancy Savoy's $25,000 donation to fund restoration and a $1,000 annual scholarship.","sources":["2005-news-sanctuarybell","2005-news-sanctuarybell-1"]}],"sources":["2005-news-sanctuarybell","2005-news-sanctuarybell-1"]},{"id":"e-t-cook","name":"E. T. Cook","aka":[],"era":"1850-1899","roles":["banker","First National Bank president"],"summary":"E. T. Cook managed the Farmers Deposit Bank in Savoy and was later promoted to the president's chair of the First National Bank of Savoy when the bank's capital was increased to $25,000 and a national charter obtained. The bank had been a paying business from its start and paid annual dividends to stockholders. It stood firmly through the money panic and was reported in good financial condition.","dates":{"born":"","died":""},"contributions":[{"text":"Managed the Farmers Deposit Bank of Savoy; promoted to president of the First National Bank of Savoy.","sources":["elk-9"]}],"sources":["elk-9"]},{"id":"madame-michely","name":"Madame Michely","aka":[],"era":"1850-1899","roles":["music teacher","professor of music"],"summary":"Madame Michely was a music teacher at Savoy College of pure Spanish stock, born, reared, and educated in Madrid. Highly accomplished in music, she played guitar, violin, harp, and piano fluently in any key. She had married a Frenchman and lived for a time in Havana, Cuba before coming to Savoy as part of a colony of educated people who intended to establish a school. When that plan failed, she made her home at Burmida's hotel in Savoy, doing private teaching and running a small store. She died a few years later from injuries sustained in the cyclone that struck Savoy. According to Lou Arterberry, she was 'one of the most intelligent women I ever knew.'","dates":{"born":"","died":"c. 1880 (in cyclone)"},"contributions":[{"text":"Came to Savoy as part of a colony planning to establish a school; provided private music instruction and taught French to local students.","sources":["college_p192"]},{"text":"Died from injuries sustained in the Savoy cyclone (likely 1880).","sources":["college_p192"]}],"sources":["college_p192"]},{"id":"mrs-jnd-mathison","name":"Mrs. E. J. Mathison","aka":["Mrs. Jnd Mathison","née Yowell"],"era":"1850-1899","roles":["music teacher","piano teacher"],"summary":"Mrs. E. J. Mathison taught private piano classes for Savoy College students during the first three years of the school's life. She was born December 21, 1840, in Henry County, Tennessee, the daughter of James A. and Sabra Yowell, and was the granddaughter of John Sevier, the first governor of the State of Franklin (later Tennessee) and a Revolutionary War hero. She married E. J. Mathison, a Northern educator and philanthropist. She left Savoy with her family in 1870, moving to Whitewright and then Nashville. She died October 18, 1926.","dates":{"born":"December 21, 1840","died":"October 18, 1926"},"contributions":[{"text":"Taught private piano classes to Savoy College students during the college's first three years, 1876-1879 approximately.","sources":["college_p193"]},{"text":"Granddaughter of John Sevier, first governor of the State of Franklin (Tennessee); brought cultivated family background to Savoy musical life.","sources":["college_p193"]}],"sources":["college_p193"]},{"id":"minnie-halsell","name":"Minnie Halsell","aka":["Miss Minnie Halsell","Mrs. William Phipps"],"era":"1850-1899","roles":["professor","primary teacher"],"summary":"Minnie Halsell was the daughter of President R. B. Halsell and received her education at Savoy College under her father's administration. She served as primary assistant teacher during the last one or two years of the college's history. Soon after the institution dissolved she married William Phipps and they moved to the Indian Territory, where she died about 1894.","dates":{"born":"","died":"c. 1894"},"contributions":[{"text":"Served as primary assistant teacher at Savoy College during its final years.","sources":["college_p192"]}],"sources":["college_p192"]},{"id":"john-s-barratt","name":"John S. Barratt","aka":["Mr. John S. Barratt"],"era":"1850-1899","roles":["professor","preparatory teacher"],"summary":"John S. Barratt was a preparatory teacher at Savoy College from 1878 until the cyclone of 1880, and came from a cultured and prominent Fannin County family. His brother T. E. Barratt was a minister of the Christian Church and editor of the Christian Messenger. Barratt had previously taught at Lamius near Dodd City, where his schoolhouse was destroyed by a storm. During the 1880 cyclone his home was wrecked one week before the main storm and his family was sheltering in a railroad section house. He subsequently became principal of the Oak Cliff high school in Dallas and died there about 1898.","dates":{"born":"","died":"c. 1898"},"contributions":[{"text":"Taught at Savoy College from 1878 until the 1880 cyclone, then became principal of Oak Cliff high school, Dallas.","sources":["college_p191"]}],"sources":["college_p191"]},{"id":"c-d-mcclellan","name":"C. D. McClellan","aka":["D. McClellan"],"era":"1850-1899","roles":["county judge","town incorporator"],"summary":"C. D. McClellan was County Judge of Fannin County, Texas in 1885. He issued the election order on May 16, 1885 calling for a vote on incorporation of the Town of Savoy, and subsequently signed the formal incorporation charter on May 6, 1885. His official acts gave Savoy its corporate legal identity with boundaries extending one quarter mile in each direction from the northeast corner of the post office lot in Block 428.","dates":{"born":"","died":""},"contributions":[{"text":"As County Judge of Fannin County, signed the incorporation charter for the Town of Savoy on May 6, 1885.","sources":["1885-docs-savoycharter"]}],"sources":["1885-docs-savoycharter"]},{"id":"sarah-malissa-deatherage","name":"Sarah Malissa Deatherage","aka":["Mrs. A. Deatherage"],"era":"1850-1899","roles":["pioneer resident"],"summary":"Sarah Malissa Deatherage was born August 8, 1856, in the Nahurin community south of Savoy, and lived in the Savoy community her entire life. She was the daughter of Mr. and Mrs. James Ray, and married A. Deatherage on August 26, 1875. Her husband died two years before her at age 94. She was a member of the Church of Christ. She died at age 88 after a two-month illness and was buried in Sunnyside Cemetery; funeral services were conducted by family friend J. B. May.","dates":{"born":"August 8, 1856","died":"c. 1944"},"contributions":[{"text":"Lifelong resident of the Savoy community from birth in 1856; member of the Church of Christ.","sources":["elk-20"]}],"sources":["elk-20"]},{"id":"j-w-woods","name":"J. W. Woods","aka":[],"era":"1850-1899","roles":["pioneer settler","farmer"],"summary":"J. W. Woods was a retired farmer born May 26, 1856, in Tennessee. He came to Texas in early manhood and married Miss Jennie Worley of Corsicana. He lived at Ector and Savoy for the last thirty years of his life and was an invalid for the last ten years. He died at age 88. Survivors included his wife, three sons, and one daughter.","dates":{"born":"May 26, 1856","died":"c. 1944"},"contributions":[{"text":"Longtime Savoy area farmer and resident; lived in Savoy for thirty years.","sources":["elk-21"]}],"sources":["elk-21"]},{"id":"h-h-arterberry","name":"H. H. Arterberry","aka":[],"era":"1850-1899","roles":["merchant","Savoy Star founder"],"summary":"H. H. Arterberry was a Savoy native educated at Savoy College who established a drug and grocery business in Savoy in 1892 when the town was nearly unpopulated. He was connected with the Savoy Tribune (later Savoy Star) newspaper for a time. He eventually took on his son Max as a partner, operating as Arterberry and Son. He and his son T. E. Arterberry are associated with the founding and long operation of the Savoy Star.","dates":{"born":"","died":""},"contributions":[{"text":"Established Savoy drug and grocery business in 1892; received education at Savoy College.","sources":["elk-24"]},{"text":"Connected with the Savoy Tribune (later Savoy Star) newspaper; his son T. E. Arterberry founded the modern Savoy Star.","sources":["elk-24"]}],"sources":["elk-24"]},{"id":"clete-stogsdill","name":"Clete Stogsdill","aka":[],"era":"2000-present","roles":["mayor"],"summary":"Clete Stogsdill served as Mayor of Savoy in 2005. He was present at the scene of the January 26, 2005 accident in which a Savoy Fire Department grass truck overturned on FM 1752, south of Savoy, injuring the driver. He and Assistant Fire Chief Michael Halford were among the officials on scene.","dates":{"born":"","died":""},"contributions":[{"text":"Served as Mayor of Savoy as of January 2005.","sources":["2005-news-firetruckwreck"]}],"sources":["2005-news-firetruckwreck"]}],"index_file":"people-index.json"};
